                      (    forth6809.asm):09495         ; TSTCOMPWORDS - compiling-words tests (glossary section 3.10,
                      (    forth6809.asm):09496         ; 13 words, 17 tests since several get separate cases: TICK
                      (    forth6809.asm):09497         ; found/not-found, ['] compiling/interpreting state, POSTPONE
                      (    forth6809.asm):09498         ; normal/immediate word, SLITERAL compiling/interpreting state,
                      (    forth6809.asm):09499         ; ABORT" false/true flag). Reuses the scratch infrastructure
                      (    forth6809.asm):09500         ; established for sections 3.8/3.9 throughout.
                      (    forth6809.asm):09501         ;
                      (    forth6809.asm):09502         ; POSTPONE is this section's hardest case: a genuine two-level
                      (    forth6809.asm):09503         ; compile-time mechanism. Traced its own code first - for a
                      (    forth6809.asm):09504         ; normal word, it compiles code that ITSELF compiles a call to
                      (    forth6809.asm):09505         ; that word later, when the current definition eventually runs,
                      (    forth6809.asm):09506         ; not immediately (standard ANS semantics: POSTPONE appends a
                      (    forth6809.asm):09507         ; word's own compilation semantics to the current definition,
                      (    forth6809.asm):09508         ; and for an ordinary word that semantics IS "compile a call to
                      (    forth6809.asm):09509         ; it", so appending it only makes sense if the current
                      (    forth6809.asm):09510         ; definition later performs that compiling action itself,
                      (    forth6809.asm):09511         ; against whatever's being compiled at that later point).
                      (    forth6809.asm):09512         ; Verified via the compiled byte sequence directly rather than
                      (    forth6809.asm):09513         ; fully executing a triple-nested compile chain.
                      (    forth6809.asm):09514         ;
                      (    forth6809.asm):09515         ; ABORT" is safely testable for both its flag cases - confirmed
                      (    forth6809.asm):09516         ; by reading its own runtime code that it uses THROW -2
                      (    forth6809.asm):09517         ; internally, not the raw, never-returns ABORT mechanism plain
                      (    forth6809.asm):09518         ; ABORT itself uses (still deliberately untested, per section
                      (    forth6809.asm):09519         ; 3.1's own reasoning).
                      (    forth6809.asm):09520         ; ------------------------------------------------------------
